---
import ContentLayout from "../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";

const categories = [
  { title: "Best Leg Workout for Beginners", href: "#beginners", description: "A simple lower-body routine for building strength, confidence, and clean form." },
  { title: "Leg Curl Machine", href: "/workouts/leg-curl-machine/", description: "Complete guide to leg curl machine setup, muscles worked, benefits, alternatives, mistakes, and hamstring training." },
  { title: "Leg Curl Machine Muscles Worked", href: "#muscles-worked", description: "The main muscles trained by leg curls and how they support stronger legs." },
  { title: "Leg Curl Alternatives", href: "#alternatives", description: "Equipment-free and gym-based movements that target the hamstrings." },
  { title: "Seated vs Lying Leg Curl", href: "#seated-vs-lying", description: "Key differences between the two common leg curl machine styles." },
  { title: "Hamstring Workout Guide", href: "#hamstrings", description: "Exercises and tips for stronger hamstrings, knees, and hip hinge power." },
  { title: "Leg Day Mistakes", href: "#mistakes", description: "Common errors that slow progress or increase injury risk on leg day." },
];
const faqs = [
  { q: "What is a good leg workout for beginners?", a: "Start with bodyweight squats, reverse lunges, glute bridges, and wall sits. Do 2–3 sets of 8–15 reps and add resistance only when your form is consistent." },
  { q: "How often should I train legs?", a: "Most people do well with 2 leg sessions per week, leaving at least one rest day between hard lower-body workouts." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="Leg Workouts — Build Strength, Muscle, and Mobility"
  h1="Leg Workouts"
  description="Free leg workout guide with beginner-friendly exercises, sample routines, and tips for building stronger quads, hamstrings, glutes, and calves."
  path="/workouts/leg-workouts/"
  lead="Train your legs with simple, effective routines for home or the gym."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <div class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
    {
      categories.map((category) => (
        <a href={category.href} class="card group p-5 transition-all hover:border-hairline-strong hover:bg-canvas-soft">
          <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink group-hover:text-brand">{category.title}</h2>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">{category.description}</p>
        </a>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="beginners">Best Leg Workout for Beginners</h2>
  <p>Leg training builds strength for daily movement, supports better posture, improves athletic performance, and helps balance your physique. Strong legs also make cardio, walking, running, and calorie-burning workouts easier.</p>

  <h2>Best leg exercises</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Squats:</strong> great for quads, glutes, and overall lower-body strength.</li>
    <li><strong>Lunges:</strong> improve single-leg strength, balance, and hip stability.</li>
    <li><strong>Romanian deadlifts:</strong> target hamstrings and glutes with a hip-hinge pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Glute bridges:</strong> simple, joint-friendly glute activation.</li>
    <li><strong>Calf raises:</strong> build calves and ankle strength.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Simple leg workout routine</h2>
  <p>Do 2–3 rounds of this routine 2 times per week:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Bodyweight or goblet squats: 10–15 reps</li>
    <li>Reverse lunges: 8–12 reps per leg</li>
    <li>Romanian deadlifts: 8–12 reps</li>
    <li>Glute bridges: 12–20 reps</li>
    <li>Calf raises: 15–25 reps</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Leg training tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Warm up first:</strong> prepare your hips, knees, and ankles before hard sets.</li>
    <li><strong>Balance your training:</strong> include squats, hinges, hamstring curls, and calf work.</li>
    <li><strong>Control every rep:</strong> smooth reps build more muscle than sloppy reps.</li>
    <li><strong>Progress gradually:</strong> add weight or reps only when your form stays consistent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Progress safely</h2>
  <p>Start with bodyweight or light resistance. Add reps first, then add weight. Keep your knees tracking over your toes, your core braced, and your back neutral. Stop if you feel sharp pain.</p>
</ContentLayout>
